Z||000007||FieroMaster88||12-02-2001||01:13 PM||||<B>BUMP!!!!</B>   <P>Anyone want to BUY a friggin 88 coupe then?!?!  I have just about had it with this stupid clutch...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||12-02-2001||02:32 PM||||Well, all I can do is point out the obvious.  That thing is one <B>beautiful</B> car.  So, yeah, I'll take it.  $50, plus you have to bring it to me down here in Wichita  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  Plus, you have to promise not to get mad when I get it working again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Please measure the stroke at the slave cylinder.  I think that's one of the surest ways to determine whether the problem is on the "outside" (hydraulics, linkage, etc.), or whether it's an "inside" job (like bent clutch fingers, fork messed up, etc.).<P>My 1988 4-cylinder Fiero has a slipping clutch - yours won't disengage.  On average, between the 2 of us, everything's just great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Maybe we need to combine the two cars into one that works  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroMaster88||12-02-2001||02:54 PM||||How much movement is the slave supposed to have?  Mine has about an inch to an inch and a half....doent seem like enough!<P>I wouldnt be so pissed off and desperate for help if this wasnt my only car.  God this sucks!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||12-02-2001||04:47 PM||||1-1 1/2 inches is more than enough. Sounds like the problem is other than the hydraulics. Bent/busted fork is a possibility (maybe caused by too much throw). I've put pedal stops on 2 of my cars to thwart that very condition.<BR>Edited to avoid grammar police<P>------------------<BR> <BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">      <BR>SC 3800<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroMaster88||12-02-2001||05:06 PM||||So you think I have a bent shift fork?  So that means I have to separate the Trans?  Damn.  I just spent an hour with a vacuum pump trying to get any and all air out of it.  There wasn't any in there, and it still moves the same.  <P>How hard is the shift fork to replace and where can I get a replacement?  And are there any other possibilities other than the shift fork?  <P>Thanks a ton for the help!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Starting to feel better because I have an idea of what the problem is!||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroMaster88||12-02-2001||10:49 PM||||BUMP...anyone else have an idea on this???||||reg||1
Z||000013||artherd||12-03-2001||02:54 AM||||Hrm, can you *CAREFUL HERE!!~!* start the car in gear? does it *MOVE* forward like the clutch is out, or does she roll?<P>Sounds like you've got good slave movement, remaining possabilities:<P>Bent external release arm<BR>Broken release shaft thing.<BR>Broken release *fork*<BR>shattered release bearing?<P>Anyway, all of these are *easially* fixable once you just drop the cradle (as though for a clutch job)  <P>The good news: YOU DO NOT HAVE TO SPLIT THE TRANNY CASE!<P>The bad news: you probally will have to drop the cradle and poke around untill you see what gave.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroMaster88||12-03-2001||07:33 AM||||Yeah..it rolls when I start it.   I know this because I still have to drive it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I will take it apart when I get the time later this week.  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000015||stsmithgt||12-03-2001||02:03 PM||||I hear you and have been there with those clutch whoes.  <BR>I have a 4SPD. clutch fork in my....trunk that let me know it was bad AFTER almost everything was replaced, Master, slave, disc, plate, bleed, bleed, bleed, and even a beat up looking fluid line to the slave.  My pedal wasn't bent.  It pisses me off it wasn't caught when all this shtuff was replaced in the first place, but hey, nobody's perfect.  Now it has a new fork, disc and plate again.  The shop didn't want to put the old plate back in because it looked bad.  NOW it shifts EXCELLENT! No stiffness or grinding second.  Only clicking into gear like it's supposed to fast or slow, high rpms or low.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Good luck with your patients.  Mine gets as thin as my wallet.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||FieroMaster88||12-03-2001||02:23 PM||||Thanks.  The only thing left to replace is the Fork assembly.  Everything else is new.  We will see what happens when I fix it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Joe Torma||12-03-2001||02:32 PM||||I tried the vac pump on my car and it didn't work for me...wasted 3 hours of my life on that.<P>What I did was use the tube with the special end hooked to the bleeder.  TooManyFieros had a good tip, use teflon tape on the bleeder threads.<P>With the tape on the bleeder threads and the tube hooked up(and draped, taped or helper holding it vertical) crack the bleeder open. Press the pedal and it should pump pure fluid up the tube.  Get to the slave cylender pull the rod into its bore as far as you can...if you see air then close off the bleeder and let the rod out.  Make sure you have enough fluid up front.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
